# Orchestration Protocol

---

## Role of the Orchestrator

The orchestrator is the **ONLY** entry point for tasks from the user.
It delegates, reviews, and merges — it does not write production code for
complex tasks.

---

## Delegation Protocol

Follow these steps in order for every dispatch:

```
Step 1 — Read planning docs (architecture, todo, backlog)
Step 2 — Overlap check (hard block if any two agents share a file)
Step 3 — Generate dispatch summary + payload (/dispatch)
Step 4 — Auto-run PowerShell dispatch
Step 5 — Monitor Discord for 🔄 start and ✅ complete posts
Step 6 — Review: git diff --name-only dev...agent-BRANCH for each agent
Step 7 — Cherry-pick clean slice commits to dev
Step 8 — Update planning docs, push to dev
```

---

## Review Protocol

Before merging any agent work to dev:

**1. Check scope**
```bash
git diff --name-only dev...agent-BRANCH-NAME
```
Must match `files_N` exactly. If extras appear — cherry-pick only the assigned-file commits.

**2. Check attribution**
```bash
git log --oneline dev..agent-BRANCH-NAME
```
Each commit must have a clear Author (`agent-name@domain`). If blank, the agent's git identity is misconfigured — fix before accepting the commit.

**3. Run tests**
Run the project test suite before pushing to dev.

**4. Integrate**
```bash
# Preferred — clean single-file slices
git cherry-pick SHA

# Only if branch is provably clean (every commit = only files_N)
git merge agent-BRANCH
```

**5. Push to dev**
```bash
git push origin dev
```

---

## Merge Policy

| Branch type | Who merges | How |
|-------------|------------|-----|
| `agent-*` → `dev` | Orchestrator only | Cherry-pick slice commits |
| `dev` → `main` | User only | PR / manual approval |

**Never merge agent branches wholesale** unless every commit in the branch
was verified to contain only the declared `files_N` paths.

---

## Parallel Work Rules

- Orchestrator may run all agents simultaneously for independent tasks
- Before parallel dispatch, verify **no file overlap** between any two agents
- Shared hotspot files must be serialized — one agent at a time
- If two agents need the same file, orchestrator sequences the work

---

## Sequenced Dispatch

When task B depends on task A completing first:

1. Dispatch Round 1 (independent agents only)
2. Wait for `✅` Discord posts from Round 1 agents
3. Dispatch Round 2 for dependent agents

Always announce the plan before Round 1:

```
⚠️ SEQUENCED DISPATCH
Round 1 → agent-01, agent-02 (parallel)
Round 2 → agent-03 (after agent-02 posts ✅)

Proceeding with Round 1 now.
```

---

## Escalation Rules

| Situation | Action |
|-----------|--------|
| Merge conflict on agent branch | Agent stops, orchestrator resolves |
| Agent blocked > 10 minutes | Agent reports, orchestrator unblocks immediately |
| Task scope grows unexpectedly | Agent reports before continuing |
| Two agents need the same file | Orchestrator serializes |
| Test failures after merge to dev | Orchestrator identifies responsible agent |
| `⚠️` Discord out-of-scope warning | Cherry-pick only — do not merge branch |

---

## Discord Channel Protocol

| Channel | Purpose |
|---------|---------|
| `#project-orchestration` | Dispatch summaries, merge announcements, force-push notices |
| `#project-agent-N` | Live start/complete updates per agent |
| `#project-blocked` | `@here` alerts — requires **immediate** response |
| `#project-deploy-monitor` | Server/deployment log alerts |

**Watch all channels after every dispatch.**
The blocked channel requires immediate response — do not let agents sit blocked.

When force-pushing to clean an agent branch, always post in `#project-orchestration`:
```
🔄 Force-push on agent-BRANCH — branch reset to origin/dev for clean dispatch
```

---

## Post-Dispatch Checklist

After all agents post `✅` in Discord:

```markdown
- [ ] Reviewed git diff for each active agent
- [ ] Cherry-picked or merged only clean slice commits
- [ ] Tests passing on dev
- [ ] Planning docs updated (tasks marked complete)
- [ ] dev pushed to origin
- [ ] Agents notified of completion
```

---

*This protocol exists to ensure every piece of agent work is traceable,
reviewable, and safe to merge. When in doubt — cherry-pick, never merge wholesale.*
